---
# Curated rather than "enable everything": mep-wm is a low-level X11/Xlib/
# D-Bus/Lua-C-API window manager, so several modernize-*/readability-* checks
# mostly flag idiomatic C-API interop (raw pointers, C-style structs, short
# geometry variable names) or fight the codebase's deliberately dense style
# (single-line `if (cond) action();` guard clauses, aligned multi-line
# ternary chains for threshold->icon lookups) rather than catch real bugs.
# bugprone-*, clang-analyzer-*, performance-*, and portability-* are enabled
# wholesale since a clean run showed they catch real issues here; readability-*
# and modernize-* are enabled too, but with the specific checks below --
# each verified false-positive/style-fight-only for this codebase -- turned
# back off:
#   easily-swappable-parameters: fires on any two adjacent same-typed params
#     (int x, int y / int width, int height, ...), which geometry code is
#     full of by nature.
#   narrowing-conversions: mostly XClientMessageEvent.data.l[n] assignments,
#     where Window/Atom (Xlib's unsigned long) going into the event struct's
#     `long` fields is the protocol's own shape, not a bug.
#   implicit-widening-of-multiplication-result: fires on small compile-time
#     literal math (25 * 60, 1024 * 1024) that can never overflow.
#   performance-enum-size: suggests shrinking enum class base types to
#     uint8_t; none of these enums are stored at scale, so it's not a real
#     saving, and int matches what they exchange with the Lua/EWMH side.
#   portability-avoid-pragma-once: mep-wm only ever targets GCC/Clang on
#     Linux (see flake.nix); portability to compilers without #pragma once
#     isn't a real constraint here.
#   security.insecureAPI.DeprecatedOrUnsafeBufferHandling: pushes C11 Annex K
#     '_s' functions (fprintf_s and friends) as the fix, but glibc -- the
#     only libc mep-wm builds against -- doesn't implement Annex K at all,
#     so there is no fix to apply.
#   readability-braces-around-statements: would force every one of the ~600
#     single-line `if (cond) action();` guard clauses onto three lines each;
#     that density is this codebase's house style, not an oversight.
#   readability-magic-numbers / readability-identifier-length: routine in
#     pixel-geometry code (padding, offsets, x/y/w/h) -- naming every 8 or 16
#     or renaming every x/y would add ceremony, not clarity.
#   readability-implicit-bool-conversion: flags idiomatic `if (ptr)` /
#     `if (count)`, which is standard, not a defect.
#   readability-math-missing-parentheses: fires on well-known, already
#     line-broken formulas (the luminance weighting in relative_luminance,
#     `y + (kBarHeight - 16) / 2`); the suggested parens are noise, not
#     disambiguation.
#   readability-avoid-nested-conditional-operator: this codebase's threshold
#     ladders (battery/volume icon selection) are deliberately written as
#     vertically-aligned `cond ? a : cond ? b : c` chains, which read as a
#     lookup table; collapsing them into if/else chains is a net readability
#     loss for that specific idiom.
#   readability-isolate-declaration: a handful of `std::string a, b, c;`
#     locals; splitting them is pure churn for no clarity gain.
#   readability-function-cognitive-complexity: real signal, but fixing it
#     means restructuring core event-dispatch functions -- an architectural
#     change out of scope for a style/lint pass, not a mechanical fix.
#   readability-named-parameter: fires on the X error handlers' intentionally
#     unnamed unused parameters (Display*, XErrorEvent*) -- naming them would
#     immediately trip -Wunused-parameter under this project's -Werror build.
#   readability-simplify-boolean-expr: its two hits both suggest De Morgan's
#     on a `!(A && B) && !(A && C)` "is this an exempted click region" guard;
#     the negated-conjunction form reads as "not in the exempted region",
#     which is clearer than the expanded disjunction.
#   modernize-use-trailing-return-type: would rewrite essentially every
#     function signature in the file for a pure style preference the rest of
#     the codebase doesn't use.
#   modernize-avoid-c-arrays: fires mostly on `constexpr const char kIcon[] =
#     "..."` string-literal constants and fixed C buffers handed to POSIX/X11
#     APIs (readlink, strftime); std::array buys nothing there.
#
# cppcoreguidelines-* is enabled the same way: its "Bounds safety" and "Type
# safety" profile checks (pro-bounds-*, pro-type-reinterpret-cast,
# pro-type-vararg, pro-type-union-access, pro-type-const-cast) assume either
# GSL types (gsl::span, gsl::at, gsl::not_null) this project doesn't depend
# on, or that C-API interop can be avoided outright -- neither holds for a
# window manager built on raw Xlib buffers, XEvent (a C union), sockaddr_un,
# and varargs POSIX calls (execl, snprintf). The rest of the group (the
# init-variables, rule-of-five, and real narrowing-conversion findings) was
# reviewed and fixed rather than suppressed -- see backend.hpp/X11Backend's
# explicit copy/move deletions and the zero-initialized out-params throughout
# x11_backend.cpp.
#   avoid-magic-numbers: same reasoning as readability-magic-numbers above.
#   pro-bounds-array-to-pointer-decay / pro-bounds-pointer-arithmetic /
#     pro-bounds-constant-array-index: fire on every `buffer[i]` / `array +
#     offset` against a raw pointer returned by Xlib (XQueryTree's window
#     list, XGetWindowProperty's property data, Xinerama's screen array) --
#     the guideline's own enforcement mechanism for this (gsl::span/gsl::at)
#     isn't part of this project's dependency set.
#   avoid-c-arrays: same reasoning as modernize-avoid-c-arrays above.
#   pro-type-reinterpret-cast: the cast between mismatched pointer types
#     these POSIX/X11 APIs require (sockaddr* <-> sockaddr_un*, unsigned
#     char* property buffers, FcChar8* for UTF-8 text) -- the APIs' own
#     shape, not a type-punning bug.
#   pro-type-vararg: fires on execl/snprintf and Xlib's own variadic-style
#     property APIs; there's no non-vararg POSIX alternative in use here.
#   pro-type-union-access: XEvent (and the XClientMessageEvent.data.l[]
#     inside it) is a C union -- that's the entire X11 event-dispatch
#     mechanism, not something mep-wm can route around.
#   pro-type-const-cast: all 4 hits are the same idiom,
#     `const_cast<XKeyEvent*>(&event)` to call XLookupString, which takes a
#     non-const event pointer purely because Xlib predates const-correctness
#     -- it doesn't write through it. The event handlers correctly take
#     `const XKeyEvent&`; the cast is the bridge, not a const-correctness bug.
#   narrowing-conversions: same XClientMessageEvent.data.l[]-shape reasoning
#     as bugprone-narrowing-conversions above (this is cppcoreguidelines' own
#     copy of that check).
# avoid-non-const-global-variables is deliberately left enabled (not in the
# list below) -- the codebase has no global variables left to except. The one
# that used to trip it (an X-error-handler flag; Xlib's error handler is a
# plain C callback with no user-data slot, so *some* storage outside the call
# stack was unavoidable) is now a function-local static reached only through
# another_window_manager_flag(), which keeps this check meaningful as a
# regression guard against a new one being added.
Checks: >
  -*,
  bugprone-*,
  clang-analyzer-*,
  performance-*,
  portability-*,
  readability-*,
  modernize-*,
  cppcoreguidelines-*,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-narrowing-conversions,
  -bugprone-implicit-widening-of-multiplication-result,
  -performance-enum-size,
  -portability-avoid-pragma-once,
  -clang-analyzer-security.insecureAPI.DeprecatedOrUnsafeBufferHandling,
  -readability-braces-around-statements,
  -readability-magic-numbers,
  -readability-identifier-length,
  -readability-implicit-bool-conversion,
  -readability-math-missing-parentheses,
  -readability-avoid-nested-conditional-operator,
  -readability-isolate-declaration,
  -readability-function-cognitive-complexity,
  -readability-named-parameter,
  -readability-simplify-boolean-expr,
  -modernize-use-trailing-return-type,
  -modernize-avoid-c-arrays,
  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-magic-numbers,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-array-to-pointer-decay,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-pointer-arithmetic,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-constant-array-index,
  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-c-arrays,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-reinterpret-cast,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-vararg,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-union-access,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-const-cast,
  -cppcoreguidelines-narrowing-conversions
WarningsAsErrors: ''
# Anchored on the project directory name rather than a bare '(src|include)/'
# -- the bare form also matches dependency headers like .../wayland-.../
# include/wayland-util.h under /nix/store, pulling their macro-heavy
# (and not ours to fix) warnings into every run.
HeaderFilterRegex: 'mep-wm/(src|include)/'
FormatStyle: none
